cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "sdmbench: reproducible benchmarking for species distribution modelling"
message: >-
  If you use sdmbench, please cite both this software AND the benchmark you
  reproduced. The benchmark's own citation is the scientifically important one;
  sdmbench is the instrument, not the result.
type: software
authors:
  - name: "sdmbench contributors"
repository-code: "https://github.com/MuhammadMuneeb007/sdmbench"
abstract: >-
  A reproducible benchmarking framework for species distribution modelling,
  ecological suitability modelling and presence-background classification. It
  separates information, representation, methodology, algorithm and validation,
  and forces every model through an identical published benchmark so that
  comparisons are fair. Includes a strict reproduction of Dinnage & Warren
  (2026), a leakage auditor, provenance tracking per protocol constant, and a
  staged methodology benchmark over modalities, scales, representations, fusion
  strategies, spatial structure and learning objectives.
keywords:
  - species distribution modelling
  - ecology
  - benchmark
  - reproducibility
  - spatial machine learning
  - presence-only
license: MIT
version: 0.1.0

references:
  - type: article
    title: >-
      A Niche in the Machine: The Promise of AI Foundation Models for Species
      Distribution Modeling
    authors:
      - family-names: Dinnage
        given-names: Russell
      - family-names: Warren
        given-names: "Dan L."
    year: 2026
    journal: EcoEvoRxiv
    doi: 10.32942/X2VQ10
    notes: >-
      The primary reproduction target. Cite this when reporting any result from
      the tabpfn-sdm-2026 benchmark.

  - type: article
    title: >-
      Presence-only and presence-absence data for comparing species distribution
      modeling methods
    authors:
      - family-names: Elith
        given-names: Jane
      - family-names: Graham
        given-names: "Catherine H."
      - family-names: Valavi
        given-names: Roozbeh
    year: 2020
    journal: "Biodiversity Informatics"
    volume: 15
    start: 69
    end: 80
    doi: 10.17161/bi.v15i2.13384
    notes: >-
      The disdat benchmark data. Cite this whenever disdat is used, which is
      every run of the tabpfn-sdm-2026 benchmark.

  - type: article
    title: "Predictive performance of presence-only species distribution models"
    authors:
      - family-names: Valavi
        given-names: Roozbeh
      - family-names: Guillera-Arroita
        given-names: Gurutzeta
      - family-names: Lahoz-Monfort
        given-names: "Jose J."
      - family-names: Elith
        given-names: Jane
    year: 2022
    journal: "Ecological Monographs"
    doi: 10.1002/ecm.1486
    notes: >-
      The source of the baseline model configurations Dinnage & Warren followed.

  - type: article
    title: "Accurate predictions on small data with a tabular foundation model"
    authors:
      - family-names: Hollmann
        given-names: Noah
      - family-names: "Müller"
        given-names: Samuel
      - family-names: Hutter
        given-names: Frank
    year: 2025
    journal: Nature
    notes: >-
      TabPFN. Cite when using any TabPFN adapter. Model weights are distributed
      under the Prior Labs License v1.1.
